{"clock":1,"nonce":"7bf2e41104414de96948d31af26ed46d","ops":[{"kind":"create","author":"ee004d31386b76440a74651c79871f156633d4ff","time":1794308753,"origin":{"forge":"github.com/guilbep/git-board","event":"issue:6305554241"},"data":{"body":"## What\n\n`docs/spec/sync.md`'s \"Nothing private published\" only covers forges. A git remote has no rule. Whatever a private forge project syncs into the board lands in `refs/board/*`, and `git board push` (or Sync's git step) sends it to origin. Nothing reads origin's visibility first (`internal/store/store.go` `Push`, `whole.go`).\n\nSo a private GitLab project, synced into a board whose origin is a public GitHub repository, publishes its issues and comments to anyone who runs `git fetch origin 'refs/board/*:refs/board/*'`.\n\nThis came from the visibility matrix (#77): in every `*-alone` cell, a private forge's issue reached the git remote and a fresh clone. That run used a local remote, so this is still to be confirmed with a public one.\n\n## To decide\n\n- Is a git remote a destination like any other, so that a private source needs `publish-private` to reach a public origin? Or is pushing the board to origin consent by design, given that the board *is* the repository's?\n- If origin counts, how is its visibility read: from the forge that hosts it, and when it can't be read?\n\n## Acceptance\n\n- [ ] The spec states the rule for git remotes.\n- [ ] The visibility matrix's `*-alone` cells check it, against a public origin.\n","title":"sync: a private forge's issues reach a public git remote through refs/board"}}]}